 },
 "paragraphs": [
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p001.s01",
     "source": "John Roberts of Colliers’ Row, Cyfartha, Merthyr, was once going to Aberdare over the mountain."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s02",
     "source": "On the top of the hill he was met by a handsome gentleman, who wore a three-cocked hat, a red waistcoat, and a blue coat."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s03",
     "source": "The appearance of this well dressed man took John Roberts’s fancy; but he could not understand why he should be alone on Aberdare mountain, and, furthermore, why he did not know the way to Aberdare, for he had asked Roberts to direct him to the town."
    },
    {
     "id": "p001.s04",
     "source": "John stared at the gentleman, and saw clearly a cloven foot and a long tail protruding underneath the blue coat, and there and then the gentleman changed himself into a pig , which stood before John, gave a big grunt, and then ran away."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p001"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p002.s01",
     "source": "I received the story from a lady to whom Roberts related it."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p002"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p003.s01",
     "source": "All these tales belong to modern times, and some of them appear to be objectless as well as ridiculous."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p003"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p004.s01",
     "source": "There are a few places in Wales which take their names from Satan."
    },
    {
     "id": "p004.s02",
     "source": "The Devil’s Bridge is so called from the tradition that it was erected by him upon the condition that the first thing that passed over it should be his."
    },
    {
     "id": "p004.s03",
     "source": "In his design he was balked, for his intended victim, who was accompanied by his faithful dog, threw a piece of bread across the bridge after which the dog ran, and thus became the Devil’s property, but this victim Satan would not take."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p004"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p005.s01",
     "source": "The Devil’s Kitchen is a chasm in the rock on the west side of Llyn Idwal, Carnarvonshire."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s02",
     "source": "The view through this opening, looking downwards towards Ogwen Lake, is sublime, and, notwithstanding its uncanny name, the Kitchen is well worthy of a visit from lovers of nature."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p005"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p006.s01",
     "source": "From the following quotation, taken from Y Gordofigion , p. 110, it would appear that there is a rock on the side of Cader Idris called after the Evil One."
    },
    {
     "id": "p006.s02",
     "source": "The words are:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p006"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p007.s01",
     "source": "“Mae ar dir Rhiwogo, ar ochr Cader Idris, graig a elwir. ‘ Careg-gwr-drwg ,’ byth ar ol y Sabboth hwnw pan ddaeth yno at drigolion plwyfydd Llanfihangel Pennant ac Ystradgwyn, pan oeddynt wedi ymgasglu i chwareu cardiau, a dawnsio; ac y rhoddodd dro o amgylch y graig gan ddawnsio, ac y mae ol ei draed ar y graig eto.”"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p007"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p008.s01",
     "source": "This in English is as follows:—There is on the land belonging to Rhiwogo, on the side of Cader Idris, a rock called The Rock of the Evil One , so named ever after that Sabbath, when he came there to join the parishioners of Llanfihangel Pennant and Ystradgwyn, who had gathered together to play cards and dance, and there he danced around the rock, and to this day the marks of his feet are to be seen in the rock."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p008"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p009.s01",
     "source": "There were, perhaps are, in Pembrokeshire, two stones, called the Devil’s Nags, which were haunted by Evil Spirits, who troubled the people that passed that way."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p009"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p010.s01",
     "source": "Ceubren yr Ellyll , the Hobgoblin’s Hollow Tree, a noble oak, once ornamented Nannau Park, Merionethshire."
    },
    {
     "id": "p010.s02",
     "source": "Tradition says that it was within the trunk of this tree that Glyndwr buried his cousin, Howel Sele, who fell a victim to the superior strength and skill of his relative."
    },
    {
     "id": "p010.s03",
     "source": "Ever after that sad occurrence the place was troubled, sounds proceeded out of the tree, and fire hovered over it, and, according to a writer in The Cambro-Briton , vol. i., p. 226:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p010"
  },
